// Fuzzel Fobble — the platform contract.
//
// The line between the two halves of this demo, and the one file both halves
// depend on. It fixes two things:
//
//   * what a frame of input looks like, in the game's vocabulary rather than
//     any library's — nine actions and a quit, no key codes;
//   * what a backend has to provide, which is the `gfx` module tabulated at
//     the foot of this file.
//
// `gfx_sdl2.fc` and `gfx_raylib.fc` each define exactly one `module gfx`, and
// a build takes exactly one of them (`sdl2.rsp` / `raylib.rsp`). FC has no
// interfaces and no traits, and this is deliberately *not* a struct of
// function pointers: the interface is a module name, the build picks which
// file defines it, and every call through it is a direct call resolved at
// compile time. The layering costs nothing at run time — which is the only
// kind of abstraction a language with no GC to foot the bill should offer.

// One frame's input, as the game understands it. `held` is the set of actions
// the player is holding right now; `tapped` is the subset that went down on
// *this* frame. Both are bit sets over the constants below.
//
// The two are different questions and the game asks both: aiming wants "is it
// held", firing wants "did it just go down" — one press should cost one
// bubble. Which of the two a backend gets for free differs (SDL2 delivers
// changes and has to be asked to remember; raylib remembers and has to be
// asked what changed), and reconciling that is the backend's job, not the
// game's.
struct input =
    quit: bool
    held: i32
    tapped: i32

    // The digit key 1-9 that went down this frame, or 0 for none. Saving and
    // loading ask "which slot", and the answer is a number: nine more action
    // bits would say the same thing at nine times the width and every reader
    // would have to turn them back into one anyway.
    //
    // A tap and never a hold. A digit held down repeating into nine saves is
    // nobody's intention.
    digit: i32

module plat =
    // The actions. A backend maps its own key codes onto these; nothing
    // downstream ever learns which key it was.
    let aim_left   = 1
    let aim_right  = 2
    let fire       = 4
    let start      = 8
    let swap       = 16
    let pause      = 32
    let symbols    = 64
    let mute       = 128
    let clear_best = 256
    let save       = 512
    let load       = 1024

    // Is this action being held? / did it go down this frame?
    let down = (inp: const input*, action: i32) -> inp.held & action != 0
    let tap  = (inp: const input*, action: i32) -> inp.tapped & action != 0

// ============================================================
// What a backend must define
//
// module gfx =
//
//   Lifecycle
//     open(lw, lh, title: const cstr)   Window, renderer and a lw x lh
//                                       logical board. Everything drawn
//                                       afterwards is in those coordinates.
//     close()                           Tear the window down. Video only —
//                                       see the note in either backend for
//                                       why the audio device is left alone.
//     set_title(t: const cstr)
//     audio_start(a: opl_audio.audio*) -> bool
//                                       Open the sound device and start its
//                                       callback. `false` = run silent.
//
//   Frame
//     pump(inp: input*)                 Fill `inp` for this frame: quit,
//                                       held, tapped, digit. Also where a backend
//                                       handles what is its own business and
//                                       not the game's — F11, for one.
//     frame_begin(cr, cg, cb)           Start a frame; clear to that colour.
//     frame_end()                       Put it on the window, letterboxed.
//
//   Primitives — logical coordinates, alpha blended, loose u8 channels
//     fill(x, y, w, h, r, g, b, a)                  filled rect
//     disc(cx, cy, rad, r, g, b, a)                 filled circle
//     hex(cx, cy, rad, r, g, b, a)                  filled hexagon, flat top
//     thick_line(x1, y1, x2, y2, th, r, g, b, a)    f64 endpoints
//
// Four primitives is the whole surface art.fc draws on. That is not a
// minimal-API exercise: it is what the two libraries genuinely agree on.
// Everything above it — the font, the bubbles, the HUD — is built out of
// these four in art.fc and is identical in both builds.
// ============================================================
